.TH DANNAN-CUSTODY 1 2026-11-15 Dannan "Dannan tools"
.SH NAME
dannan-custody \- make and check a node's key custody under a systemd credential
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dannan-custody init
.B \-\-state
.I DIR
.B \-\-credential
.I NAME
.br
.B dannan-custody show
.B \-\-state
.I DIR
.B \-\-credential
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-credentials-dir
.IR DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A node's private key and seal root are held in key custody,
.IR DIR /identity/custody,
sealed under a root that is never on disk in clear. The root reaches the node
as the systemd credential
.IR NAME ,
which the unit decrypts into
.B $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY
at each start.
.B dannan-custody
runs on the node, with the node stopped: both commands refuse while a node
holds the state directory's lock
.RI ( DIR /.dannan.lock),
saying the path is in use.
.TP
.B init
Makes custody for the state directory
.IR DIR :
a fresh node key and seal root under a new root. The new root is printed once,
as 64 hex digits and a line end, and nothing else is written to standard
output, so it may be piped straight to
.BR systemd-creds (1)
.BR encrypt .
It is never stored in clear: pipe it straight into
.BR "systemd-creds encrypt" ,
never to a file, which would keep the root on disk in clear. Standard output closed is refused before anything
is made. If the root cannot be written (a full device, or its reader gone), the
custody just made is removed and init exits 1, so it may run again. If the root was written but
.B systemd-creds encrypt
then failed, nothing can open the custody: remove
.IR DIR /identity/custody
and run init again. The node's id,
.IR DIR /node-id,
is made with it, as a node makes it at its first start; an id already there is
kept. Custody that exists is never replaced: init then refuses and changes
nothing.
.TP
.B show
Opens custody with the credential as the unit will find it and prints the
node's id and public key as one JSON line,
.BR {"id":"...","public_key":"..."} ,
for
.BR dannan-ca (1)
.BR issue .
It proves the sealed credential opens custody before the node is started on
it, and writes nothing: a directory with no node id, mistyped or fresh, is
refused. A root that does not open custody, or a custody file altered, is
refused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-state " DIR"
The node's state directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-credential " NAME"
The systemd credential's name, as the unit's
.B LoadCredentialEncrypted=
names it.
.TP
.BI \-\-credentials-dir " DIR"
For show: where the decrypted credential is, instead of
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY .
.SH SEALING WITHOUT A TPM
Where the host has no TPM,
.B systemd-creds encrypt
seals the root with the host's key,
.IR /var/lib/systemd/credential.secret ,
alone. A root sealed that way opens only on the host that sealed it. An
off-node backup must keep either the plain root, offline, or the sealed root
together with that host's
.IR credential.secret ;
the sealed root without it cannot be opened anywhere else, and custody with it.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
systemctl stop node
dannan-custody init --state /var/lib/node --credential dannan-root \\
  | systemd-creds encrypt --name=dannan-root - /etc/credstore.encrypted/dannan-root
systemd-run --wait --pipe -p LoadCredentialEncrypted=dannan-root \\
  dannan-custody show --state /var/lib/node --credential dannan-root
systemctl start node
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when custody cannot be made or opened (custody that exists,
the state in use, a wrong root, a missing credential), 2 for a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dannan-ca (1),
.BR dannan-import-macha (1),
.BR dannan-version (1),
.BR systemd-creds (1)
